## Quick‑look

| Feature | `std::optional<T>` | `std::variant<T1, T2, …>` |
|---------|-------------------|--------------------------|
| **What it is** | “Maybe‑a‑value” container. It can be *empty* or hold a single `T`. | “One‑of‑many” container. It holds **exactly one** value, which can be any of the listed types. |
| **Typical use** | Return a value that might not exist (e.g., a lookup that can fail). | Model a type that can be one of several alternatives (sum type) – e.g., a function that can return an int *or* an error string. |
| **Check contents** | `.has_value()`, `.value_or(default)`, `operator bool()` | `std::holds_alternative<Ti>(v)`, `std::get<Ti>(v)`, `.index()`, or `std::visit` |
| **Default state** | Empty (`std::nullopt`) | First alternative by default (unless you use `std::monostate` to have an “empty” state). |

Below are *minimal* examples that illustrate the two concepts side‑by‑side.

---

## 1. `std::optional`

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

// Try to parse an integer from a string.
// If parsing succeeds, return the value; otherwise return std::nullopt.
std::optional<int> try_parse_int(const std::string& s)
{
    std::istringstream iss{s};
    int x;
    if (iss >> x && iss.eof())   // everything consumed and no error
        return x;                // holds a value
    return std::nullopt;         // no value
}

int main()
{
    std::string inputs[] = {"42", "hello", "3.14", "7"};

    for (auto& in : inputs)
    {
        auto maybe = try_parse_int(in);

        if (maybe)                           // same as maybe.has_value()
            std::cout << "Parsed " << in << " -> " << *maybe << '\n';
        else
            std::cout << "Could not parse " << in << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Key take‑aways**

| Operation | What it does |
|-----------|--------------|
| `std::nullopt` | A sentinel representing “no value”. |
| `opt.has_value()` or `if (opt)` | Test if it contains a value. |
| `opt.value()` | Return the value or throw `std::bad_optional_access` if empty. |
| `*opt` or `opt.value()` | Dereference to get the stored `T`. |
| `opt.value_or(default)` | Return the value, or a default if empty. |

