## Putting them together – a tiny “Maybe‑or‑Error” helper

Sometimes you want “maybe a value” **or** “an error”. A clean way to express this is:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <variant>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// Either we get a value or an error string
using Result = std::variant<std::optional<int>, std::string>;

// Dummy function: looks up an index in a vector
Result get_element(const std::vector<int>& v, std::size_t idx)
{
    if (idx < v.size())
        return std::optional<int>{v[idx]};   // value present
    else
        return std::string{"index out of bounds"}; // error
}

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> data{10, 20, 30};

    for (auto idx : {1, 5})
    {
        auto r = get_element(data, idx);
        std::visit([](auto&& val){
            using T = std::decay_t<decltype(val)>;
            if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, std::optional<int>>)
                if (val)
                    std::cout << "value = " << *val << '\n';
                else
                    std::cout << "value = nullopt\n";
            else
                std::cout << "error: " << val << '\n';
        }, r);
    }
}
```

The pattern is:

1. **`std::optional<T>`** → “value may or may not exist”.
2. **`std::variant<...>`** → “one of several possibilities”.

Using them together yields expressive, type‑safe APIs without resorting to exceptions or error codes.

---

### TL;DR

| What you need | Choose |
|---------------|--------|
| A value that *might* be missing | `std::optional<T>` |
| A value that can be *one of many types* | `std::variant<T1,T2,…>` |
| A value that *might be missing* *or* an *error* | `std::variant<std::optional<T>, ErrorType>` (or a dedicated result type) |

Both are part of the C++17 standard library, available in all modern compilers, and make your code safer and clearer by encoding “missing” or “multiple‑choice” semantics directly into the type system.
